.TH CLAUDE-SYNC 1 "2026-10-11" "agent-tools" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
claude-sync \- two-way sync of Claude Code sessions between machines
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B claude-sync sync
.I host
[\fB\-\-project\fR \fIDIR\fR | \fB\-\-all\fR] [\fB\-\-account\fR \fIname\fR] [\fB\-\-config\fR] [\fB\-n\fR] [\fB\-\-allow\-deletes\fR]
.br
.B claude-sync mv
[\fB\-\-account\fR \fIname\fR] [\fB\-n\fR]
.I old new
.br
.B claude-sync split
[\fB\-\-to\fR \fIname\fR] [\fB\-\-from\fR \fIname\fR] [\fB\-n\fR]
.I dir
.br
.B claude-sync split \-\-all
[\fB\-\-from\fR \fIname\fR] [\fB\-n\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B claude-sync
syncs Claude Code sessions with
.I host
over
.BR ssh (1)
and
.BR rsync (1),
so a session started on one machine can be continued with
.B claude \-\-resume
on the other. Both machines must use the same home directory and project
paths. The script sends itself to
.I host
on each run, so only
.BR python3 ,
.B rsync
and
.BR flock (1)
need to be installed there.
.PP
Transcripts are append-only: the longer copy wins when the other is a prefix
of it. When a session was continued on both machines, the two continuations are
chained into one conversation and both originals are kept next to it as
.IB name .conflict\- host \- hash
files. Other files are compared against the state of the last sync with
.IR host :
a file changed on one side is copied, a file deleted on one side is moved to
.I ~/.claude/claude\-sync/trash
on the other, and a file changed on both sides keeps the local version and
saves the remote one as a conflict copy.
.I history.jsonl
and memory indexes are merged.
.PP
Sessions open on the receiving side, or busy on the sending side, are skipped
and reported with their pid and tmux pane.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-project " DIR"
Sync the sessions of the project in
.I DIR
(default: the current directory), plus
.IR history.jsonl .
.TP
.B \-\-all
Sync every project.
.TP
.BI \-\-account " name"
Sync this account instead of the project's; see
.BR ACCOUNTS .
.TP
.B \-\-config
Also sync
.IR CLAUDE.md ,
.IR settings.json ,
.I keybindings.json
and
.IR skills/ .
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-dry\-run
Print the plan and change nothing.
.TP
.B \-\-allow\-deletes
Allow a sync that deletes more than 20 files and more than a quarter of the
files in scope; without it such a sync is refused.
.SH MOVING A PROJECT
.B claude-sync mv
renames the directory
.I old
to
.I new
and updates Claude Code's records of it, so its sessions can still be resumed
from the new path: the project directories under
.I ~/.claude/projects
for
.I old
and every directory inside it, the
.B cwd
of each record in their transcripts,
.I history.jsonl
and the per-project settings and GitHub repo paths in
.IR ~/.claude.json .
Paths mentioned in the conversations themselves are left as they were. If
.I old
is already gone and
.I new
exists, only the records are updated. It refuses while a session is running
in
.I old
or
.IR new ,
and
.B \-n
prints what it would do.
.PP
Run it on both machines before the next sync. Run on one only, a sync with
.B \-\-all
would delete the old project's files on the other machine.
.SH ACCOUNTS
With accounts set up by
.BR claude\-user (1),
each command works on one account's Claude dir:
.I ~/.claude
for the default account,
.I ~/.claude\-name
for the others. It is the account named with
.BR \-\-account ,
else the one the project directory belongs to: with
.BR \-\-all ,
the current directory's; for
.BR mv ,
.IR old 's.
.B sync
passes the account's name to
.IR host ,
which finds its Claude dir from its own config, so the account that owns
.I ~/.claude
can differ between the machines. The account's dir must exist on both. Without
a config, everything is in
.IR ~/.claude ,
as before.
.SH SPLITTING ACCOUNTS
.B claude-sync split
moves sessions out of one account's Claude dir, by default the default
account's
.IR ~/.claude ,
into another's: what is needed to sort a
.I ~/.claude
that held every account before
.BR claude\-user (1)
was set up. With
.IR dir ,
the sessions of
.I dir
and the directories inside it go to
.IR dir 's
account, or to the one named with
.BR \-\-to .
With
.BR \-\-all ,
every project goes to the account its directory belongs to; projects already
there stay. A project's directory is the one its transcripts record; for a
project without any, such as one holding only memory, it is the one directory
of that name in
.IR history.jsonl ,
else the one existing directory whose name the project's encodes to, and the
project is skipped if neither tells.
.B \-\-from
names the account to move out of.
.PP
For each project it moves the project directory under
.I projects
(transcripts, subagents, tool results, memory), each session's
.IR file\-history ,
.I session\-env
and
.I todos
entries, the project's prompts in
.IR history.jsonl ,
and its settings and GitHub repo paths in
.IR .claude.json .
A project directory the target already has is merged into, file by file; a
file in both that differs stops the split before anything moves. Settings stay
where they are if the target account has no
.I .claude.json
yet: run Claude Code in it once first. It refuses while a session is running
in a project being moved, and
.B \-n
prints what it would do.
.PP
Run it on both machines before the next sync of either account. Run on one
only, the syncs still move the sessions on the other machine, as deletions from
one account and new files in the other (a large split needs
.BR \-\-allow\-deletes ),
but there the prompts come back to the source account's
.I history.jsonl
and the settings stay in its
.IR .claude.json .
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B CLAUDE_SYNC_MACHINE_ID
Identifies this machine in the sync state when
.I /etc/machine\-id
is missing.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.claude/claude\-sync/
Lock, per-peer sync state, trash and staging, on each machine, in each
account's Claude dir.
.TP
.I ~/.cache/claude\-sync/hash\-cache.json
Cached file hashes.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR agent\-ps (1),
.BR claude\-user (1),
.BR rsync (1),
.BR ssh (1)
